## Code Conventions

The FE and BE stacks each have their own conventions. The two subsections
below carry only the rules that apply to this specific repo. For the
richer, ready-to-adapt baseline (Vue / TanStack Query / Pinia on the FE,
.NET / EF Core / AutoMapper on the BE), copy from:

- [`asdd/ai-workflow/templates/AGENTS.md.fe.example`](../asdd/ai-workflow/templates/AGENTS.md.fe.example)
  — the full FE `## Code Conventions` section.
- [`asdd/ai-workflow/templates/AGENTS.md.be.example`](../asdd/ai-workflow/templates/AGENTS.md.be.example)
  — the full BE `## Code Conventions` section (with its `Layering & DTO
  placement / API conventions / Error envelope / Persistence &
  migrations / Style` subsections).

### Frontend

<Fill in with the rules that actually apply to your FE side. Examples:>

- **Composition style** — <e.g. Composition API only, React function
  components + hooks, Angular standalone components>.
- **HTTP layer** — <all HTTP goes through `<useHttpClient / apiClient /
  fetcher>`; components never call `axios` / `fetch` directly>.
- **State** — <Pinia / Redux / Zustand / NgRx — as declared here>.
- **Routing / route metadata** — <feature-flag gate, access-level gate,
  code-splitting rule>.
- **Errors** — <typed error classes + global error UX>.
- **Style / formatter** — <Prettier / ESLint config, print width,
  quote style>.
- **Comments:** the agent honors the minimal-comments rule
  ([`asdd/ai-workflow/AGENTS.md` → Rule #6](../asdd/ai-workflow/AGENTS.md)).

### Backend

<Fill in with the rules that actually apply to your BE side. Examples:>

- **Layering discipline** — <Controller → Service → Repository → ORM>.
  No DB calls from controllers. No business logic in controllers.
- **DTO placement** — Web DTOs in `<path>`; Core DTOs in `<path>`.
  Never leak an entity through the HTTP boundary.
- **Mapping** — <AutoMapper profile / MapStruct mapper / manual mapper>.
- **Auth** — every endpoint carries `<[Authorize(Policy = …)] / guard /
  decorator>` unless it is genuinely public.
- **API conventions** — <routing style, response type attributes,
  idempotency policy>.
- **Error envelope** — <shape + mapping table>. Throw the typed
  exception; the global handler applies the envelope.
- **Persistence & migrations** — <DbContext location, migrations flow,
  schema-change gating>.
- **Style / analyzer** — <formatter, analyzers, warnings-as-errors
  policy>.
- **Comments:** the agent honors the minimal-comments rule
  ([`asdd/ai-workflow/AGENTS.md` → Rule #6](../asdd/ai-workflow/AGENTS.md)).

### Cross-boundary

- **Contract source of truth:** `<OpenAPI file | protobuf | shared
  types>`. Both sides consume it; neither hand-writes duplicate types.
- **Envelope alignment:** the FE error classes (`<...>`) map 1:1 to the
  BE error envelope statuses (`<...>`). Adding a new BE status ⇒ add the
  matching FE error class in the same ticket (or split into two coupled
  tickets with an explicit `blocks` link).
- **Cross-package imports:** BE ⇢ FE imports are forbidden. FE ⇢ BE
  runtime imports are forbidden (only shared package imports are
  allowed).

## Commit & PR Conventions

- **Base branch:** `development` (or per `base_branch` in the frontmatter).
- **Branch name convention:** `<prefix>/<TICKET-ID>-<short-slug>` off the
  base branch. Prefix follows the change type (`feature`, `fix`, `chore`,
  `refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `perf`, `ci`, `build`, `revert`).
- **Commit format** — Conventional Commits with the ticket ID:

  ```text
  <type>(<scope>): <lowercase description> #<TICKET-ID>
  ```

  - **Allowed types:** `feat | fix | docs | style | refactor | test |
    chore | perf | ci | build | revert`.
  - Scope names the affected side or package
    (e.g. `feat(web): …`, `fix(api): …`, `chore(shared): …`).
  - Message references the ticket ID.
- **One ticket → one focused PR.** A single PR MAY touch both FE and BE
  if the ticket's slice is inherently cross-boundary (e.g. adding an
  endpoint and its consumer in one vertical slice); otherwise split.
- **PR checks** — <name the CI workflows that must be green>.
- **No `--force` on shared branches. No `--no-verify`.**

## What NOT to Do

- **Do not** run `<install> / <build> / <test> / <lint> / <migrate> /
  git *` commands. List them in the delivery summary and let the
  Developer run them.
- **Do not** introduce cross-boundary imports (BE importing FE code, or
  FE importing BE code outside the shared package).
- **Do not** duplicate types across FE and BE — extend the shared
  contract instead.
- **Do not** hand-write a bespoke error shape on either side; both go
  through the declared envelope.
- **Do not** add inline comments to generated code except when genuinely
  necessary, when they carry important non-obvious context, or when the
  user explicitly asked. Only a **file-header block of at most 3 lines**
  may be added on the agent's initiative. See
  [`asdd/ai-workflow/AGENTS.md` → Rule #6](../asdd/ai-workflow/AGENTS.md)
  for the full rule; do not restate it here.
- **Do not** silence warnings, delete tests, narrow scope, or bypass
  auth guards to make a check pass. See
  [`asdd/ai-workflow/AGENTS.md` → Rule #2](../asdd/ai-workflow/AGENTS.md).
- **Do not** merge a ticket that lacks a resolved effective profile
  (see the top of this file and
  [`asdd/ai-workflow/ASDD.md` → Profiles](../asdd/ai-workflow/ASDD.md#profiles)).
- **Do not** push directly to the base branch or force-push shared
  branches. **Do not** commit with `--no-verify`.
